WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.861
days later the rainstorm ended.

2
00:00:18.022 --> 00:00:22.203
The wind stopped howling, and a brilliant blue sky stretched over the moor.

3
00:00:23.184 --> 00:00:28.566
The moor itself looked soft blue instead of gloomy purple-black or awful, dreary gray.

4
00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:33.008
When Mary awoke she sat up in bed and called to Martha.

5
00:00:33.536 --> 00:00:34.456
"Look at the moor!"

6
00:00:34.476 --> 00:00:38.999
"Yes," said Martha with a cheerful grin.

7
00:00:39.019 --> 00:00:41.740
"The storm's over for a bit.

8
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:43.741
That happens this time of year.

9
00:00:44.341 --> 00:00:45.682
Spring is on its way."

10
00:00:45.702 --> 00:00:50.204
"I thought it always rained and looked dark in England," said Mary.

11
00:00:50.224 --> 00:00:52.165
"Oh, no.

12
00:00:52.665 --> 00:00:58.808
Just you wait until you see all the flowers blooming on the moor and butterflies fluttering and bees

13
00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:04.127
humming. You'll want to go out on the moor all day as Dickon does."

14
00:01:05.328 --> 00:01:06.209
Mary smiled.

15
00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:09.411
"I like Dickon, and I've never even met him."

16
00:01:09.431 --> 00:01:12.033
"All the animals like Dickon.

17
00:01:12.614 --> 00:01:16.657
I wonder," said Martha, "what he would think of you."

18
00:01:16.677 --> 00:01:20.881
"He wouldn't like me," Mary said stiffly.

19
00:01:20.901 --> 00:01:22.282
"No one does."

20
00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:24.864
Martha looked serious.

21
00:01:25.484 --> 00:01:26.706
"How do you like yourself?"

22
00:01:27.886 --> 00:01:29.408
Mary hesitated for a moment.

23
00:01:30.032 --> 00:01:30.993
"Not at all, really.

24
00:01:31.453 --> 00:01:33.354
But I never thought of that before."

25
00:01:34.455 --> 00:01:35.195
Martha grinned.

26
00:01:35.796 --> 00:01:40.358
"Mother asked me that once when I was in a bad temper and speaking ill of everyone.

27
00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:46.502
She said, 'There you stand saying you don't like this one and you don't like that one.

28
00:01:47.442 --> 00:01:48.643
How do you like yourself?'

29
00:01:49.224 --> 00:01:51.965
It made me laugh and brought me to my senses."

30
00:01:53.286 --> 00:01:56.408
Martha was in high spirits that morning because it was her day off.

31
00:01:57.588 --> 00:02:01.131
She gave Mary her breakfast, and then she was going home to the cottage.

32
00:02:02.352 --> 00:02:04.434
She wouldn't be back until the next morning.

33
00:02:06.295 --> 00:02:09.158
After Martha left, Mary felt lonelier than ever.

34
00:02:10.279 --> 00:02:15.303
She went outside, and the first thing she did was run around the fountain ten times.

35
00:02:16.444 --> 00:02:20.347
Then she ran into the kitchen gardens and found Ben Weatherstaff at work.

36
00:02:21.388 --> 00:02:23.750
The change in the weather seemed to have done him good.

37
00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:27.437
"Springtime's coming," he said cheerfully.

38
00:02:27.457 --> 00:02:28.558
"Can you smell it?"

39
00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:30.800
Mary sniffed the air.

40
00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:34.763
"I smell something nice and fresh and damp."

41
00:02:34.783 --> 00:02:38.766
"That's the good, rich earth," he said, digging away.

42
00:02:38.786 --> 00:02:43.190
"In the flower gardens, things will be stirring down there in the dark.

43
00:02:44.351 --> 00:02:45.492
The sun's warming them.

44
00:02:45.912 --> 00:02:47.894
You'll see bits of green sticking up soon."

45
00:02:47.914 --> 00:02:50.416
"What will they be?"

46
00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:53.899
"Crocuses and snowdrops and daffodils.

47
00:02:54.419 --> 00:02:55.260
Haven't you ever seen them?"

48
00:02:56.388 --> 00:02:57.829
"No," said Mary.

49
00:02:57.849 --> 00:03:02.311
"In India everything is hot and wet and green after the rains.

50
00:03:03.392 --> 00:03:05.213
Things there seem to grow up overnight."

51
00:03:05.233 --> 00:03:09.395
"These won't grow up in a night," said Ben.

52
00:03:09.415 --> 00:03:11.556
"You'll have to wait for them.

53
00:03:11.917 --> 00:03:15.418
They'll poke up a bit higher here and push out a bit more there.

54
00:03:16.579 --> 00:03:19.621
Then they'll uncurl a leaf this day and another one that day.

55
00:03:20.161 --> 00:03:20.681
You watch."

56
00:03:21.862 --> 00:03:22.662
"I'm going to."

57
00:03:23.163 --> 00:03:28.126
Mary heard the soft rustling of wings, and there was the robin looking right at her.

58
00:03:28.146 --> 00:03:30.628
"Do you think he remembers me?"

59
00:03:30.648 --> 00:03:33.550
"Remember you?"

60
00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:34.571
said Ben.

61
00:03:34.591 --> 00:03:36.192
"Of course he does."

62
00:03:36.212 --> 00:03:39.374
"Are things stirring in the garden where he lives?"

63
00:03:40.475 --> 00:03:41.276
"What garden?"

64
00:03:41.696 --> 00:03:42.697
Ben said gruffly.

65
00:03:42.717 --> 00:03:45.379
"The one where the old roses are."

66
00:03:45.939 --> 00:03:48.181
Mary couldn't help asking more questions.

67
00:03:48.201 --> 00:03:50.742
"Are all the flowers dead?

68
00:03:51.223 --> 00:03:52.504
Are there ever any roses?"

69
00:03:53.996 --> 00:03:54.837
"Ask the robin.

70
00:03:55.037 --> 00:03:56.217
He's the only one who knows.

71
00:03:56.758 --> 00:03:58.419
No one else has seen it for ten years."

72
00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:01.300
Ten years was a long time.

73
00:04:01.901 --> 00:04:03.421
She was ten years old herself.

74
00:04:04.642 --> 00:04:07.264
Mary slowly walked away, lost in thought.

75
00:04:08.424 --> 00:04:13.047
She had begun to like the garden, just as she had begun to like Dickon and Martha and

76
00:04:13.127 --> 00:04:13.967
their mother too.

77
00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:18.670
That seemed a lot of people to like when you weren't used to liking people.

78
00:04:19.811 --> 00:04:21.792
She also counted the robin as a person.

79
00:04:22.888 --> 00:04:27.891
She spotted him again as she was strolling along her favorite path outside the kitchen gardens.

80
00:04:29.032 --> 00:04:32.134
He was pecking in the earth and pretending that he hadn't followed her.

81
00:04:32.574 --> 00:04:33.794
But Mary knew he had.

82
00:04:33.814 --> 00:04:36.456
"You do remember me!"

83
00:04:36.916 --> 00:04:37.497
she cried.

84
00:04:38.697 --> 00:04:42.259
She chirped and talked and coaxed, while he hopped and twittered.

85
00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:46.242
The robin allowed her to move closer and closer to him.

86
00:04:47.422 --> 00:04:50.044
She bent down and tried to make robin sounds.

87
00:04:51.564 --> 00:04:53.445
The robin hopped under some shrubs.

88
00:04:54.625 --> 00:04:59.186
Then Mary saw him stop to look for a worm near a pile of freshly turned-up dirt.

89
00:05:00.286 --> 00:05:02.347
An animal must have been digging in the garden.

90
00:05:03.467 --> 00:05:08.349
As she watched the robin, she spotted something half-buried in the little pile of soil.

91
00:05:09.469 --> 00:05:12.390
It looked like a ring of brass or rusty iron.

92
00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:17.811
When the robin flew up into a nearby tree, Mary picked up the ring.

93
00:05:18.872 --> 00:05:19.872
But it wasn't a ring.

94
00:05:20.444 --> 00:05:21.826
It was an old key.

95
00:05:22.967 --> 00:05:25.550
Mary stared in amazement at the key in her hand.

96
00:05:26.732 --> 00:05:30.236
"Perhaps it has been buried for ten years," she whispered.

97
00:05:30.256 --> 00:05:33.100
"Perhaps this is the key to the garden."
